  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Sunday) \nIssued at 1045 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nDry conditions with above normal warmth for early October continues\nThursday. Surface high pressure situated from the central Great\nPlains to off of the Mid Atlantic coast should bring an easterly \nflow across the Deep South. Under mostly sunny skies, warm\ntemperatures will prevail with highs rising into the mid/upper 80s.\nThis warmth will result in low afternoon relative humidities reduced\ninto the mid 20s percent across much of the Tennessee Valley west of\nI-65. A forecast of light winds should help temper elevated outdoor \nfire spread risks. Similar warmth is forecast Fri with highs again\nclimbing into the mid/upper 80s, with similar reduced mid afternoon\nrelative humidities. Low temperatures both Thu/Fri night should cool\ninto the 50s.\n\nA forecast for the upcoming weekend is one of low confidence\nregarding rain chances. The models generally show moisture to our\nsouth moving further northward. Some of the models, especially the\n12Z Canadian and 18Z GFS hint at a potential tropical system moving\nfrom the western Gulf of America, across the Deep South (including\nthis region) Sat into Sun. The 12Z ECMWF kept it's tropical entity\nmore over the western Gulf, with lesser moisture impacting this\nregion. Many things could occur with the development of this system\nas we go through the week; something that'll closely be monitored.",
